 for bestpractices and # Login Page Prompt for AI Development Tool

This prompt is designed to drive an AI development tool to build a complete, production-ready Login Page within the LifeOps ecosystem. It enforces runtime safety, security best practices, and a consistent design system as outlined in the PROJECT CONTEXT.

---

## Login Page

## Overview
Create the Login Page as the entry point for user authentication. It must securely support both email/password sign-ins and OAuth sign-ins (Google, GitHub, Microsoft), integrate with the app’s session management, provide a 2FA option, and handle common authentication flows (remember me, forgot password, and navigation to signup). The page should be visually consistent with LifeOps’ dark-mode first design system and support accessibility best practices.

---

## Page Description (Full Detail)
What this page is:
- A dedicated authentication entry point for returning users, offering multiple authentication methods (email/password and OAuth providers) and clear pathways for account recovery and onboarding.

Goals:
- Safely authenticate returning users with minimal friction.
- Provide secure session initialization and 2FA flow options if the user has 2FA enabled.
- Guide users toward password reset or signup when needed.
- Maintain a consistent look and feel with the LifeOps UI system, including typography, spacing, color palette, and component primitives.

Connected features:
- User Authentication: Robust authentication with email/password, OAuth, session management, and 2FA.
- Security notices: Clear indicators for security best practices and prompts for 2FA enrollment if applicable.

UI elements and how they should look:
- Email field with accessible label and validation feedback.
- Password field with show/hide toggle and strong focus state.
- Remember me checkbox with accessible label.
- Forgot password link styled as an inline secondary action.
- OAuth sign-in buttons for Google, GitHub, and Microsoft with brand-accurate icons.
- Sign-in primary button in LifeOps blue (#4F8CFF) with disabled state when inputs are invalid.
- Sign-up link for new users.
- 2FA prompt option (conditional): If the user account has 2FA enabled or required, show a 2FA flow trigger after initial credential verification (e.g., enter TOTP or pass a prompt confirmation).
- Security notices: Small, unobtrusive messages about best practices and security constraints.
- Visual guidance: Card-based, left-aligned content with generous spacing, consistent with a 12-column grid. Cards have rounded corners, soft shadows, and subtle borders. Hover states on interactive elements provide visual feedback.

UI/UX guidance:
- Dark-mode-first with high-contrast text, subdued placeholders, and accent-driven actions.
- Clear success/error states with accessible text for screen readers.
- Keyboard navigable with logical focus order.
- Minimal cognitive load: progressive disclosure for 2FA, helpful inline validation, and concise copy.

---

## Components to Build
- EmailPasswordLoginCard
  - Email input: type="email", required, validation (format + presence).
  - Password input: type="password", with show/hide toggle, required.
  - RememberMeCheckbox: controlled boolean; value persists in localStorage if enabled.
  - ForgotPasswordLink: navigates to Password Reset page.
  - SignInButton: primary action, disabled until form is valid.
  - SignUpLink: navigates to Signup page.
  - Inline ValidationMessages: per-field feedback with accessible aria-live regions.

- OAuthSignInSection
  - GoogleSignInButton
  - GitHubSignInButton
  - MicrosoftSignInButton
  - Each button displays brand icon, label, and starts OAuth flow via backend API endpoints.

- TwoFAInlineFlow (conditional)
  - Triggered after credential validation if the account has 2FA enabled or required.
  - TOTPTInput: for TOTP codes; optional push-notification or backup code support if defined.
  - ResendCodeButton (if applicable)
  - Verify2FAButton: submits 2FA code to verify and establish session.

- SecurityNotice
  - Small, non-intrusive text about security best practices and 2FA importance.

- ProviderAuthModal (optional)
  - If OAuth sign-in requires user to choose a provider in a modal on smaller screens.

- PasswordResetRedirect
  - Lightweight component or route link to Password Reset flow.

Note: All components must guard against null/undefined values before invoking array methods or manipulating data. Use data ?? [] and Array.isArray checks where applicable. Initialize React state with proper defaults (e.g., useState<string>("") for strings, useState<boolean>(false) for checkboxes, useState<T[]>([]) for arrays). Ensure all API response shapes are validated before use.

---

## Implementation Requirements

### Frontend
- Pages:
  - LoginPage (React route: /login)
- UI Components:
  - EmailPasswordLoginCard
  - OAuthSignInSection
  - TwoFAInlineFlow (conditionally rendered)
  - SecurityNotice
- State Management:
  - useState and/or context for form data, validation states, loading indicators, and 2FA flow state.
  - Persist "Remember me" preference in localStorage (or secure equivalent) with safe defaults.
- Form Validation:
  - Email regex validation (or built-in HTML5 validation) with accessible error messages.
  - Password: non-empty and any length constraints defined by security policy (e.g., min 8 chars).
  - Debounced validation to avoid excessive re-renders (optional).
- Accessibility:
  - All inputs have associated labels, aria-invalid, aria-describedby for errors, and keyboard accessible controls.
- Theming:
  - Style using the given color palette, typography, and card/grid system. No gradients beyond flat blocks; ensure contrast ratios meet accessibility standards (WCAG AA or better).
- Interactions:
  - Show/hide password toggle.
  - Hover and focus states on all interactive elements.
  - OAuth flows trigger backend endpoints and handle redirects securely; handle any error states gracefully.
- Routing & Navigation:
  - On successful login, redirect to Master Dashboard (e.g., /dashboard) with a secure session.
- Security considerations:
  - Do not store plaintext passwords; use secure tokens/sessions.
  - Implement client-side input validations and server-side enforcement.
  - 2FA prompts should only appear after secure credential verification, not preemptively.

### Backend
- Endpoints (to be consumed by frontend but described for completion):
  - POST /auth/login
    - Body: { email, password, rememberMe }
    - Returns: { sessionToken, user, requires2FA }
  - POST /auth/login/oauth
    - Body: { provider } or initiate OAuth flow and redirect to provider
  - POST /auth/verify-2fa
    - Body: { email, code } or via session context
    - Returns: { sessionToken, user }
  - POST /auth/logout
  - POST /auth/password-reset/request
  - POST /auth/password-reset/confirm
- Data persistence:
  - Sessions stored securely with appropriate TTLs.
  - 2FA status per user (enabled/required).
- Security:
  - Ensure rate limiting on login endpoints to prevent brute force.
  - Use CSRF protections and secure HttpOnly cookies where applicable.
- API response shapes:
  - Always validate: const data = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : [].

### Integration
- Frontend should call:
  - Email/password login to /auth/login
  - OAuth initiation to /auth/login/oauth with provider
  - 2FA verification to /auth/verify-2fa
  - Password reset to /auth/password-reset/* routes
- State flow:
  - Initial: render login form with OAuth options.
  - On submit: show loading; if requires2FA is true, render TwoFAInlineFlow; else redirect to /dashboard.
  - On OAuth: redirect to provider and handle callback to establish session securely.
- Security and data flow:
  - Never expose tokens in URL query strings; use secure redirects and set tokens in HttpOnly cookies if possible.
  - Use proper error handling with user-friendly messages without leaking sensitive info.

---

## User Experience Flow
1. User lands on Login Page with options:
   - Email field, Password field (with show/hide), Remember me, Forgot password, Sign in button.
   - OAuth options: Google, GitHub, Microsoft.
   - Sign up link for new users.
2. User enters valid email and password and clicks Sign In.
3. If credentials are valid and 2FA is not required, user is redirected to the Master Dashboard (/dashboard) and a session is created.
4. If 2FA is required, the UI transitions to the TwoFAInlineFlow:
   - User enters the TOTP code or completes the 2FA prompt and submits.
   - On success, user is redirected to /dashboard.
5. If credentials are invalid, an accessible error message is displayed and inputs remain focused for correction.
6. If the user clicks Forgot password, redirect to Password Reset Page to initiate email-based reset.
7. If the user clicks Sign up, redirect to the Signup Page.
8. Visual feedback:
   - Loading indicators during API calls.
   - Inline validation messages for invalid inputs.
   - Clear success/error states with screen-reader friendly announcements.

---

## Technical Specifications

Data Models:
- User
  - id: string
  - email: string
  - passwordHash: string (server-side only)
  - twoFAEnabled: boolean
  - twoFARequired: boolean
  - oauthProviders: string[] (e.g., ["google","github","microsoft"])
  - createdAt: string
  - updatedAt: string
- Session
  - id: string
  - userId: string
  - token: string (server-side, or HttpOnly cookie)
  - expiresAt: string
- AuditLog (optional for run artifacts)
  - id: string
  - action: string
  - userId: string
  - timestamp: string
  - details: string

API Endpoints:
- POST /auth/login
  - Body: { email, password, rememberMe }
  - Response: { ok: boolean, sessionToken?: string, requires2FA?: boolean, user?: User, error?: string }
- POST /auth/login/oauth
  - Body: { provider }
  - Response: redirect to provider or { ok: true, sessionToken, user } after callback
- POST /auth/verify-2fa
  - Body: { code }
  - Response: { ok: boolean, sessionToken?: string, user?: User, error?: string }
- POST /auth/logout
  - Response: { ok: boolean }
- POST /auth/password-reset/request
  - Body: { email }
  - Response: { ok: boolean }
- POST /auth/password-reset/confirm
  - Body: { token, newPassword }
  - Response: { ok: boolean }

Security:
- Use proper CSRF protection and same-site cookies where applicable.
- Do not expose sensitive data in client-side logs or responses.
- Enforce minimum password policies server-side; client should reflect policy guidance.
- Implement account lockout or exponential backoff after repeated failed attempts.
- 2FA mechanisms should be extensible (TOTP, WebAuthn if supported).

Validation:
- Client: Email format, password non-empty, rememberMe boolean.
- Server: Validate input shapes; use schema validation (e.g., Zod, Joi) on API inputs.
- All API responses must validate shapes; handle nulls gracefully:
  - const loginData = Array.isArray(payload?.data) ? payload.data : [];
  - const sessionToken = payload?.sessionToken ?? undefined;
  - Use Optional chaining: user?.id, etc.

Error Handling:
- Show concise, non-technical messages for user-facing errors.
- Log detailed errors server-side for troubleshooting only.

---

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] The Login Page renders with email/password fields, show/hide toggle, remember me, forgot password, and Sign in button; OAuth options render for Google, GitHub, and Microsoft.
- [ ] Validation prevents submission with empty fields; meaningful inline errors shown.
- [ ] Successful email/password login creates a session and redirects to /dashboard; 2FA flow triggers when required and completes with successful redirect.
- [ ] OAuth sign-ins initiate provider flow and return to an authenticated session; errors are handled gracefully.
- [ ] Password reset flow is accessible from the Login Page and completes a reset via token link.
- [ ] All API responses and client data handling use nullish coalescing and array guards as specified.
